 # 003 — Tap, listen and sound-button components
 
-Status: done — PR #3
+Status: done — PR #3 · reviewed by Claude 2026-09-25
 Owner: Kimi · Reviewer: Claude, then DQ
 
 ## Goal
@@ -81,3 +81,13 @@ One page showing: a ChoiceGrid with 2, 3, 4 and 6 options (and the result state)
 - `SoundButtons` renders letters at 72px in fixed 76px cells; words up to ~7 graphemes fit portrait iPad at the demo's max-w-3xl. Longer words (PH-16 style) may need a smaller variant later — flag for the phonics screen brief.
 - `speech.ts` prefers en-GB (Daniel/Kate/Serena on iPad), falls back to any en-*; voices load async on Safari — `primeVoices()` handles `voiceschanged`. Rate 0.85 default.
 - Next.js 16: read `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` per AGENTS.md — client-component conventions unchanged from what the code uses.
+
+## Review (Claude, 2026-09-25)
+Accepted. Good work — lint, tests and build pass, layout fits iPad portrait/landscape. Fixed in review:
+- **Hydration error (React #418) on `/dev/components`.** `SpeakButton` called `canSpeak()` during render, so the server HTML (nothing) and the first client render (a button) differed. Now uses `useSyncExternalStore` with a `false` server snapshot.
+- **`speak()` could hang on iOS.** Safari sometimes never fires `onend`, leaving the button pulsing forever. Added a watchdog timeout so the promise always settles, and kept a reference to the live utterance (Chrome can garbage-collect it mid-speech).
+- **Split digraph placement.** The final `e` was always put at the very end of the word, so `b·i-e·k·s` would render "biks e". It now goes straight after the next grapheme ("bikes"). The `e` letter is taken from the grapheme, not hard-coded.
+- **Arc overlapped the markers.** The split-digraph arc ran through the dot under `k` in "cake" and merged with the `ch` line in "ache". Dots and lines now sit higher, and the arc dips below them.
+- Demo page: added `bikes` and `ache` to show those cases.
+
+Not changed, for later: `SoundButtons` is fixed at 72px letters (7 letters ≈ 530px), which is fine for iPad. The phonics screen may need a `size` prop for phones.
